<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<network-security-config>
    <!--
        Cleartext (HTTP) traffic is permitted here for ONE reason: LibreChat is self-hosted, and
        people run it on their own network without a certificate — http://192.168.1.20:3080,
        http://localhost:3080, http://nas.local. That is the only case plain http is meant for.

        This resource cannot say so. Its rules are domain names (with an includeSubdomains flag),
        not address ranges: there is no way to write « 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 100.64/10,
        fd00::/8, loopback » in a <domain-config>, and the server address is whatever the user
        typed. A base-config of cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" would therefore cut every LAN
        server off at the OS level, with no way to allow it back.

        So the platform policy stays permissive and the bounded rule lives in the app instead
        (security review of 26/09/2026, finding M4), in three places that all read one definition
        of « private host », core/common's CleartextPolicy:

          - the server URL screen refuses an http:// address whose host is public — no dialog,
            because there is no informed « yes » to sending the password across the internet in
            the clear; a private host still gets the existing warning dialog;
          - the engine settings form applies the same rule to the engine, portal and scheduler
            addresses (finding F5);
          - CleartextGuardPlugin, installed on every Ktor client, refuses an http:// SEND to a
            public host — which is what covers an absolute http:// URL the server hands back (a
            download URL, an avatar) and a redirect towards one, neither of which passes through
            an entry screen.

        Net effect: https anywhere, http only towards loopback, the RFC 1918 and CGNAT ranges,
        link-local, IPv6 unique-local, the .local/.lan/.home/.internal/.home.arpa suffixes and a
        single-label name. A local server therefore keeps working exactly as before, warning dialog
        included; a public http:// server is refused at entry and, should one slip through by any
        other path, at send time.

        No certificate pinning: the deployment is user-chosen, so there is no certificate to pin
        at build time. Per-server pinning would belong in the `servers` table (see core/data).
    -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
